      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. Her surrounding remarks distinguish the depth of that love from her inability or unwillingness to voice it extravagantly.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"since, I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The expression affirms an existing filial bond rather than creating a new promise. “According to my bond” explicitly positions Cordelia's love as governed by her obligation as Lear's daughter, and her next turn identifies the associated duties as obedience, love, and honour. This contextual material qualifies the focal avowal itself, rather than constituting an unrelated promise. The commitment is substantial but not dominant because Cordelia is principally answering a question about how much she loves him, not undertaking a newly defined future course.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression is a measured, deliberately limited answer to Lear's demand, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged exclamation. Cordelia explicitly frames herself as unable to turn her heart into extravagant speech.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth:\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and bond-affirmation, the expression functions as a principled correction to Lear's competitive love test: Cordelia limits her claim to the proper measure of filial love and rejects the implication that a daughter should profess exclusive or unlimited love for her father.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to declare how much they love him while distributing portions of his kingdom. Cordelia answers Lear directly, refusing the expansive rhetoric used by her sisters and defining her love by the proper filial bond.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms that she is bound to Lear by filial duties encompassing love, obedience, and honour; she does not make a new future-tense promise. Lear does not accept this bounded affirmation as an adequate response: he urges her to revise it and then rejects and disinherits her when she maintains its truth.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied source is William Shakespeare’s King Lear, and the focal expression is Cordelia’s response during Lear’s love test in Act I, Scene I.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied text strongly supports avowal, genuine affection, and affirmation of an existing filial obligation. The precise practical scope of that obligation is only partly specified, and Lear’s hostile response establishes rejection of her formulation rather than absence of undertaking force.\"}"
        }
      ],
